  rulesHTML: `<p>Take turns placing marks, win the small boards and make three in a row on the main board. But you don't choose where to play: your move decides where your opponent goes.</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Warm-up.</b> First a classic 3×3 game, X starts. The winner gets a ★ to start with, the loser moves first in the match. On a draw nobody gets a star and a coin decides the first move.</li>
<li><b>Where to play.</b> A move in cell <b>N</b> sends your opponent to board <b>N</b> (it's highlighted). The first move, a free move, and a move when you're sent to a full board can go to any board.</li>
<li><b>Small boards.</b> Three in a row and the board is yours for good: your mark goes onto the main board, and the loser gets a free move. You can keep playing in a won board. A draw makes the main board cell neutral.</li>
<li><b>Stars.</b> Every board you win gives you a ★.<br><b>Navigator, 1★:</b> place a star instead of a mark (it counts as your mark) and choose which board your opponent goes to.<br><b>Strike, 2★:</b> instead of a move, remove an opponent's mark in the board you were sent to, if nobody has won it yet. Your opponent goes to the board matching the removed cell.</li>
<li><b>Victory.</b> Three in a row on the main board. If no row is possible anymore, play on: whoever has more marks on the main board wins. Equal means a draw.</li>
<li class="mystery"><b>???</b> Unknown. Find it yourself. There is a way to win even when the main board is already lost.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Nostalgia</h3><p>Classic tic-tac-toe: three in a row horizontally, vertically or diagonally. X starts.</p>
<h3>Bots</h3><p>All the bots are strong. The names are honest.</p>`
